  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n- Valley fog tonight into Thursday morning for Northwest Montana.\n \n- Warming and drying trend Friday through Sunday.\n \n- Increasing westerly winds and potential Monday afternoon storms\n  as a weak disturbance flattens the ridge. \n\n- Strong high pressure re-establishes late next week, maintaining\n  a prolonged warm and dry pattern. \n \nTonight-Thursday: Over the past 24 hours, a weak atmospheric river\nbrought widespread precipitation to areas north of the Highway 12\ncorridor, with a band from Noxon to Logan Pass picking up between \n0.50 and 1.00 inch of rain. The increased low-level moisture \ncombined with light winds will likely promote valley fog \ndevelopment tonight into Thursday morning. High-resolution \nguidance continues to show a weak disturbance brushing northwest \nMontana Thursday, which could produce another round of light \nshowers or sprinkles with accumulations generally limited to a few\nhundredths of an inch.\n\nFriday-Sunday: An upper-level ridge of high pressure will build\nacross the Northern Rockies Friday through Sunday. This pattern \nwill bring a distinct warming and drying trend across all valleys \nand terrain through the weekend.\n\nMonday-Next Week: Long-range guidance indicates a weak disturbance\nsliding east along the Canadian border Monday into Tuesday,\ntemporarily flattening the upper ridge. This system will lead to\nan uptick in westerly winds across the Northern Rockies. A few\nmodels also suggest sufficient destabilization for isolated\nthunderstorm development by Monday afternoon. Once this\ndisturbance clears out, a strong ridge of high pressure will re-\nbuild over the region for the remainder of next week and\npotentially even longer, prolonging warm and dry conditions.",
